**assistant**:
> It looks like `latexdiff` is still processing the content of your sidewaystable despite the exclusion command. To ensure that it ignores not just specific commands but also the content of those environments completely, you can use additional options.
> 
> 1. **Use `--exclude-textcmd`:** Double check that you're using the right syntax for `sidewaystable` specifically, as sometimes the environment might be recognized differently.
> 
> 2. **Use `--exclude-environments`:** Instead of just excluding commands, you can use the `--exclude-environments` option to tell `latexdiff` to ignore any changes within specific environments.
> 
>    ```bash
>    latexdiff --exclude-environments="sidewaystable" v1/main.tex v2/main.tex > diff.tex
>    ```
> 
> This will ensure that all the content within `sidewaystable` is ignored for the diffing process, without generating any `\DIFdelbegin` and `\DIFdelend` commands in that environment.
> 
> 3. **Combining Both:** If needed, you can combine both methods to exclude both commands and environments:
> 
>    ```bash
>    latexdiff --exclude-textcmd="table,tabular" --exclude-environments="sidewaystable" v1/main.tex v2/main.tex > diff.tex
>    ```
> 
> 4. **Review the Output:** After running one of the commands above, check the generated `diff.tex` file. If you still find any `\DIFdel` or `\DIFadd`, it might be worthwhile to open the output file and confirm whether the `sidewaystable` was indeed included correctly in your original LaTeX files. 
> 
> Following these steps should help resolve the issue. Let me know if you need further assistance or adjustments!
